# SYNOPSIS

```usage
od [-bdosvx] [-A addressformat] [-E | -e] [-j skip] [-t outputformat] [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`od` reads each <file> in order, as one stream, and writes its bytes on standard output as lines of numbers or characters.
With no <file>, or for a <file> of `-`, it reads standard input.

Each line starts with the offset of its first byte in the stream, then shows the bytes that follow, interpreted as each type asked for.
A line holds 16 bytes, or the least common multiple of the types' sizes, doubled until it is 16 or more.
When more than one type is asked for, each shows the same bytes on a line of its own, the offset written only on the first.
A short last line is padded with zero bytes to a whole value of each type.
A last line holds the offset after the last byte: the length read.
With no type, `od` shows the bytes as octal 4-byte words.

: `-A` <addressformat>
  Write offsets in decimal (`d`), octal (`o`, the default) or hexadecimal (`x`), seven digits at least; or not at all (`n`).
: `-b`
  As `-t o1`: octal bytes.
: `-d`
  As `-t u2`: unsigned decimal 2-byte words.
: `-o`
  As `-t o2`: octal 2-byte words.
: `-s`
  As `-t d2`: signed decimal 2-byte words.
: `-x`
  As `-t x2`: hexadecimal 2-byte words.
: `-E`, `-e`
  Read words of more than one byte as big-endian, or as little-endian.
  The default is the machine's: little-endian on x86_64 and arm64.
: `-j` <skip>
  Skip the first <skip> bytes of the stream.
  <skip> is decimal, octal with a leading `0` or hexadecimal with a leading `0x`, and may end in `b`, `k`, `m` or `g` for units of 512, 1024, 1024² or 1024³ bytes.
: `-N` <count>
  Read no more than <count> bytes after those skipped, written as <skip> is.
: `-t` <outputformat>
  Show the bytes as each type <outputformat> lists, in order.
  `a` is a named character, its high bit ignored, such as `nl` or `sp`; `c` is a character, a C escape such as `\n`, or three octal digits.
  `d`, `o`, `u` and `x` are signed decimal, octal, unsigned decimal and hexadecimal 4-byte words.
  Bytes and 2-byte words are had with the flags above.
: `-v`
  Accepted and ignored: every line is written, and a run of identical lines is never folded into `*`.

`od` is sbase's, built into `sbase-box` with sbase's other commands and installed as `/boot/bin/posix/od`, a hard link to it.
In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)) `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so POSIX programs find it as `/bin/od`.
There is no native `od`.

# EXAMPLES

As `tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs it in the POSIX namespace, with `a` and a newline on standard input:

```text
% echo a | od -b
0000000 141  12
0000002
```

# SEE ALSO

namespace(6); sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/od.1` in the repository

# DIAGNOSTICS

Messages go to standard error, after the name `od` was run by.
A malformed flag, address format, type or offset prints the usage message, after `parseoffset` <skip>`: invalid value` or the like for an offset, and exits 1.
A <file> that cannot be opened is reported as `od: open` <file>`:` and the error, and the next is read; a failed read is reported as `od: read` <file>`:` and the error.
The exit status is 1 if any <file> failed or the output could not be written, otherwise 0.

# BUGS

`-N` works but is left out of the usage message.

With no type, POSIX asks for octal 2-byte words, as `-o` gives; `od` shows 4-byte words.

Signed types are not sign-extended: `-s` shows the bytes `ff ff` as `65535`, not `-1`.
A size after a type in `-t`, as in POSIX's `-t x2` or `-t dL`, is refused with the usage message: the size is read, then read again as a type.
The floating-point type `f` is refused too.

`-E` also left-justifies each value in its column, which `-e` does not.
